   uunnccttrrll
       The uunnccttrrll routine returns a character string which is a printable rep‐
       resentation of the character _c_h:

       •   Printable characters are displayed as themselves, e.g., a one-char‐
           acter string containing the key.

       •   Control characters are displayed in the ^^_X notation.

       •   Printing characters are displayed as is.

       •   DEL (character 127) is displayed as ^^??.

       •   Values  above 128 are either meta characters (if the screen has not
           been initialized, or if mmeettaa(3X) has been called with a TTRRUUEE param‐
           eter),  shown  in the MM--_X notation, or are displayed as themselves.
           In the latter case, the values may not be printable;  this  follows
           the X/Open specification.

       The  corresponding wwuunnccttrrll returns a printable representation of a com‐
       plex character _w_c_h.

       In both uunnccttrrll and wwuunnccttrrll the attributes and color associated with the
       character parameter are ignored.

   kkeeyynnaammee,, kkeeyy__nnaammee
       The kkeeyynnaammee routine returns a character string corresponding to the key
       _c.  Key codes are different from character codes.

       •   Key codes below 256 are characters.  They are displayed using  uunncc‐‐
           ttrrll.

       •   Values  above 256 may be the codes for function keys.  The function
           key name is displayed.

       •   Otherwise (if there is no corresponding name and the key is  not  a
           character)  the  function returns null, to denote an error.  X/Open
           also lists an “UNKNOWN KEY” return value,  which  some  implementa‐
           tions return rather than null.

       The  corresponding kkeeyy__nnaammee returns a multibyte character string corre‐
       sponding to the wide-character value _w.  The two functions (kkeeyynnaammee and
       kkeeyy__nnaammee) do not return the same set of strings:

       •   kkeeyynnaammee returns null where kkeeyy__nnaammee would display a meta character.

       •   kkeeyy__nnaammee does not return the name of a function key.

   ffiilltteerr,, nnooffiilltteerr
       The  ffiilltteerr  routine, if used, must be called before iinniittssccrr or nneewwtteerrmm
       are called.  Calling ffiilltteerr causes these changes in initialization:

       •   LLIINNEESS is set to 1;

       •   the capabilities cclleeaarr, ccuudd11, ccuudd, ccuupp, ccuuuu11,  ccuuuu,  vvppaa  are  dis‐
           abled;

       •   the capability eedd is disabled if bbccee is set;

       •   and the hhoommee string is set to the value of ccrr.

       The  nnooffiilltteerr  routine  cancels  the effect of a preceding ffiilltteerr call.
       That allows the caller to initialize a screen on  a  different  device,
       using  a  different  value of $$TTEERRMM.  The limitation arises because the
       ffiilltteerr routine modifies the in-memory copy of the terminal information.

   uussee__eennvv
       The uussee__eennvv routine, if  used,  should  be  called  before  iinniittssccrr  or
       nneewwtteerrmm  are  called (because those compute the screen size).  It modi‐
       fies the way _n_c_u_r_s_e_s treats environment variables when determining  the
       screen size.

       •   Normally  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  looks  first  at  the  terminal database for the
           screen size.

           If uussee__eennvv was called with FFAALLSSEE for parameter, it stops  here  un‐
           less uussee__ttiiooccttll was also called with TTRRUUEE for parameter.

       •   Then  it  asks  for the screen size via operating system calls.  If
           successful, it overrides the values from the terminal database.

       •   Finally (unless uussee__eennvv was called with FFAALLSSEE  parameter),  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s
           examines  the _L_I_N_E_S or _C_O_L_U_M_N_S environment variables, using a value
           in those to override the results from the operating system or  ter‐
           minal database.

           _c_u_r_s_e_s also updates the screen size in response to SSIIGGWWIINNCCHH, unless
           overridden by the _L_I_N_E_S or _C_O_L_U_M_N_S environment variables,

   uussee__ttiiooccttll
